WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.385
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.710
SARA ROBINSON: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.482
Thank you for coming.

00:00:08.482 --> 00:00:10.690
Today we're going to talk
about machine learning APIs

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:11.524
by example.

00:00:11.524 --> 00:00:12.940
I'm going to teach
you how you can

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.860
access pre-trained
machine learning models

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.780
with a single API call.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.100
My name is Sara Robinson.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.650
I'm a developer advocate on
the Google Cloud Platform

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.800
team, which basically means I
get to help build demos, give

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.740
talks about them, and
bring product feedback back

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.360
to the engineering teams.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.700
You can find me on
Twitter, @SRobTweets.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.390
And I live in New York.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.890
So before we get
started, let's talk

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.160
about what machine learning
is at a high level.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.060
So at a high level,
machine learning

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.220
is teaching computers
to recognize patterns

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.200
in the same way
that our brains do.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.150
So it's really easy for
a child to recognize

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.542
the difference between a
picture of a cat and a dog,

00:00:48.542 --> 00:00:50.500
but it's much more
difficult to teach computers

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:51.910
to do the same thing, right?

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.381
So we could write rules to
look for specific things,

00:00:54.381 --> 00:00:56.380
but we can almost always
find a condition that's

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.795
going to break those rules.

00:00:57.795 --> 00:00:59.170
So instead, what
we want to do is

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.570
write code that finds these
rules for us and improves

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:06.040
over time through
examples and experience.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.290
Here we have a
neural network that's

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.246
identifying a picture as either
a picture of a cat or a dog.

00:01:11.246 --> 00:01:13.120
And we can think of the
input to this network

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.740
as pixels in the image.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.870
And then each neuron
is looking for

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.820
a specific identifying feature.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.449
Maybe it's the shape
of the ear or the hair.

00:01:21.449 --> 00:01:22.990
And then the output
is a prediction--

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.405
in this case, that is a dog.

00:01:26.405 --> 00:01:28.280
Let's take a step back
from this for a moment

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.650
and let's try some
human-powered image detection,

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.290
if we were to do
this on our own.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.660
We'll take this picture
of an apple and an orange.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.160
And let's say we
were going to start

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.840
writing an algorithm
that would identify

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.420
the difference
between these two.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.050
What are some features that
you might look for you?

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.096
You can shout it out.

00:01:43.096 --> 00:01:43.870
AUDIENCE: Color.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.155
SARA ROBINSON: Color.

00:01:45.155 --> 00:01:46.280
I heard a bunch of "color."

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:46.995
AUDIENCE: Shape.

00:01:46.995 --> 00:01:47.870
SARA ROBINSON: Shape.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.080
AUDIENCE: Texture.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.180
SARA ROBINSON: Texture--
lots of good ones.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.680
So color's a good
one, but then what

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.640
would happen if we had
black and white images?

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.590
Then we might have to
start all over again.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.070
So in that case, we
could for a stem.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.110
Texture would be good.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.900
But then what happens
if we add a third fruit?

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.468
If we add a mango, we have to
start all over again as well.

00:02:06.468 --> 00:02:08.509
But these pictures are
all pretty similar, right?

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:13.197
So what would happen if we had
pictures of two things that

00:02:13.197 --> 00:02:14.030
were very different?

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.446
This should be
really easy, right?

00:02:15.446 --> 00:02:17.600
A dog and a mop have pretty
much nothing in common

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.330
from these pictures
that I can see.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:24.540
But it's actually
a little tricky.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.169
So what we have here is
pictures of sheep dogs and mops.

00:02:28.169 --> 00:02:30.710
And it's actually kind of hard
to tell the difference, right?

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:33.560
If we were going to write code
that identified these two,

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.640
it would be pretty tricky to do.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.179
And then what happens if we
have photos of everything?

00:02:40.179 --> 00:02:41.720
We don't want to
write specific rules

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.050
to identify each little thing
that we're trying to look for.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.740
And in addition to photos, we
could have many other types

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.090
of unstructured data.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.940
We could have
video, audio, text,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.587
lots of different types of
data we'd be dealing with.

00:02:55.587 --> 00:02:57.170
And really, we want
some tools to help

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.920
us make sense of all of
this unstructured data.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.200
And Google Cloud Platform
has several products

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.720
to help you benefit
from machine learning.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.260
On the left-hand side here,
you can use your own data

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.060
to build and train your
own machine learning model.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.680
So we have TensorFlow and Cloud
Machine Learning Engine for you

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.502
to do that.

00:03:16.502 --> 00:03:17.960
And on the right-hand
side, this is

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.460
where I'm going to focus today.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.880
This is what I like to call
friendly machine learning.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.890
These are machine learning
APIs that give you

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:28.520
access to a pre-trained
machine learning model with one

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.410
single REST API request.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.790
So you make a request.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.170
You send it some data.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.559
You get some data back from
this pre-trained model.

00:03:35.559 --> 00:03:37.850
And you don't have to worry
about building and training

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.019
your own model or anything
that's going on under the hood.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:42.560
I'm going to give
you an introduction

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.970
to each of these five APIs.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.779
I'm going to start
with Vision, and I'm

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:47.570
going to end with Video
Intelligence, which

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:52.370
is our newest API you may have
seen in the keynote yesterday.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.460
So let's get
started with Vision.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.680
The Vision API lets you
do complex image detection

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.522
with a simple REST request.

00:03:59.522 --> 00:04:00.980
And I'm going to
start each section

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.710
by talking about a
customer or customers

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.840
that are using each API.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.690
So for the Vision API, the
first example on the left

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.780
is Disney used the
Vision API for a game

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.510
to promote the movie
"Pete's Dragon."

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.600
And the way the game worked is
that users were given a quest.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.790
So they had a clue,
and they had to take

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.620
a picture of that word--

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.650
maybe couch, computer,
everyday objects.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.370
And if they took the
picture correctly,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.291
they would superimpose an image
of the dragon on that object.

00:04:30.291 --> 00:04:31.790
So the problem was
they needed a way

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.480
to verify that the user took a
picture of the correct object

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.111
that they were prompted for.

00:04:37.111 --> 00:04:39.110
And the Vision API was a
perfect fit to do that.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.320
They used the label detection
feature, which basically tells

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.820
you, what is this a picture of?

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:47.040
And they were able to verify
images in the game that way.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.350
Realtor.com uses the Vision API
for their mobile application.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.260
So its a real estate
listing service, and people

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.180
can go around as they
are looking for houses

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.760
and take pictures of
a "for sale" sign.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.580
And they use the Vision
API's OCR, Optical Character

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.250
Recognition, to read
the text in the image

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.790
and then pull up the relevant
listing for that house.

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:12.417
So those are two examples of
the Vision API in production.

00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:14.750
Let's talk a little bit more
about the specific features

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.259
of the Vision API.

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:18.050
So as I mentioned, we
have label detection,

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.675
which is kind of the
core feature which

00:05:19.675 --> 00:05:20.900
tells-- you send it an image.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.805
In this case, it's a cheetah.

00:05:22.805 --> 00:05:24.680
And it'll tell you what
this is a picture of.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.460
It'll give you a bunch
of different labels back.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.860
Face detection will
identify faces in an image.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.490
It'll tell you where those
faces are in the image.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.140
And it'll even tell you if
they're happy, sad, surprised,

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.850
or angry.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:42.010
OCR is what I mentioned
with realtor.com's use case.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.580
This can identify
text in an image.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.460
It will tell you where the
text is, what the text says,

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.500
and what language it's in.

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.020
Explicit content
detection will tell you,

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.814
is this image
appropriate or not--

00:05:52.814 --> 00:05:54.230
really useful if
you've got a site

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.210
with a lot of
user-generated content

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:58.250
and you don't want to
manually filter images.

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:02.690
You can use this API method
to do that really easily.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:06.080
Landmark detection will tell
you, is this a common landmark?

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.510
If so, what is the
latitude and longitude?

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.580
And then logo detection,
pretty self-explanatory,

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.015
will identify logos in an image.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.570
So a quick look at some
of the JSON response

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.510
you might get back for
these different features.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:22.530
This is face detection.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.170
This is actually a selfie
that I took with two teammates

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.480
on a trip to Jordan last year.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.890
And the response you're looking
at in the slide is on my face.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.640
So it'll return an
object for each face

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.410
you find in the image.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.467
And we can see it says, headwear
likelihood, very unlikely,

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:38.050
which is true.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:39.240
I'm not wearing a hat.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.970
But for both of my teammates, it
did return headwear likelihood,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:42.759
very likely.

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:44.550
And then we can see
it's highlighted below.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.970
It says, joy likelihood is
very likely, which is true.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.240
I am smiling in the picture.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.010
The next feature I want to
show you the response for

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.540
is landmark detection.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.760
So we have a
picture here of what

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.230
looks like the Eiffel Tower.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:02.860
It's actually the Paris Hotel
and Casino in Las Vegas.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:06.910
I wanted to see if the
Vision API was fooled.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:07.780
And it was not.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:12.280
It correctly identified this
as the Paris Hotel and Casino.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.310
You can see that MID
in the JSON response.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.620
That's an ID that maps to
Google's Knowledge Graph

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.290
API, which will just give
you a little more data

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:21.900
about the entity.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.480
And it also tells us the
latitude and longitude of where

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.730
this Paris Hotel and Casino is.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.920
In addition to these features,
we launched some new features

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.378
this week, which
you may have heard

00:07:34.378 --> 00:07:35.950
about in yesterday's keynote.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:37.866
I'm going to quickly
talk about what they are.

00:07:37.866 --> 00:07:39.680
And then I'll show
you some examples.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:41.350
The first one is
crop hints, which

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.480
will give you suggested crop
dimensions for your photos.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.399
Web annotations-- I'm super
excited about this one.

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:48.940
This will give you
some granular data

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:52.810
on entities, web entities
that are found in your image.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.780
It'll also tell you all the
other pages where the image

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:57.080
exists on the internet.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.410
So if you need to do
copyright detection,

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:02.500
it'll give you the URL
of the image and the URL

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.170
of the page where the image is.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:09.040
And then finally, we announced
document text annotations.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.310
So in addition to the OCR we
had before, this is improved

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.410
OCR for large blocks of text.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.894
If you have an image of
a receipt or something

00:08:16.894 --> 00:08:18.310
with a lot of text,
it'll give you

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.060
very granular data on the
paragraphs and the words

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.944
and the symbols in that text.

00:08:25.944 --> 00:08:27.360
Some examples of
the new features,

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.310
I want to highlight
web annotations.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.220
So here we have a
picture of a car.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.547
It's in a museum.

00:08:33.547 --> 00:08:35.130
I'm actually a big
"Harry Potter" fan,

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.890
so this is a car from one of
the "Harry Potter" movies.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.539
And I wanted to
see what the Vision

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:44.625
API was able to find from the
web entities in this image.

00:08:44.625 --> 00:08:46.250
So it was able to
identify it correctly

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:47.850
as a Ford Anglia,
which is correct.

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.120
This is from the second
"Harry Potter" movie when they

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.080
tried to fly a car to school.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.536
Second entity it returned
is art science museum.

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:57.910
And this is a
museum in Singapore

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.666
where this car is on display.

00:09:01.666 --> 00:09:03.040
And it is finally
able to tell me

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.620
that this car is from
"Harry Potter," which

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:07.360
is a literary series.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.720
So lots of great metadata
you can get back from web

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.610
annotations.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.650
Even more data that it returns--

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.690
it tells you the full
matching image URLs.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:19.960
Where else does this image
exists on the internet?

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.860
Partial matching images.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.460
And then finally, all the
pages that point to this image.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.890
It's really useful
information you get back

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:30.127
with web annotations.

00:09:30.127 --> 00:09:32.460
And you can try this in the
browser with your own images

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.260
before writing any code.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.930
Just go to
cloud.google.com/vision.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:38.790
You can upload your
images, play around,

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.207
see all the responses
you get back

00:09:40.207 --> 00:09:41.373
from the different features.

00:09:41.373 --> 00:09:43.560
And you can actually do
this with all of the APIs

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.610
that I'm going to
talk about today.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.470
There's a way to try
them in the browser.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:51.810
And in case you were wondering
how the Vision API did

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:55.140
with the sheep dogs and mops,
so this is the response it got

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.790
from that picture on the right.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.680
So it's 99% sure it's a dog.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.760
It actually even is able to
identify the breed of the dog--

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:02.270
Komondor.

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:05.510
I may be saying that wrong.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:07.550
And the mops, it
successfully identified this

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.650
as a broom or a tool.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:12.560
And skipping ahead, it
did pretty well overall.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.990
In the top row, the third one,
it didn't identify it as a dog.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.200
It just said "fur."

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.010
So I don't know if
that's a hit or a miss.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.380
And then the third
mop, it said "textile."

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:24.920
So it didn't quite get that
it was a mop or a broom.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.170
But overall, Vision API
performed pretty well

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:30.950
on these tricky images that are
even hard for us to decipher

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:31.985
what they are exactly.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:36.870
So that was the
Vision API showing you

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.390
how you can get a lot
of data on your images

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.560
with a pre-trained
machine learning model.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.160
Next I want to talk
about audio data.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.640
And the Speech API essentially
exposes the functionality

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.615
of "OK, Google" to developers.

00:10:49.615 --> 00:10:53.220
It lets you do speech-to-text
transcription in over 80

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.050
languages.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.020
One app that's using the
Speech API is called Azar.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.820
And they have
connected 15 million

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:03.910
matches, an app to
find friends and chat.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:07.000
And they use the Speech
API for all of the messages

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.370
that involve audio snippets.

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:11.380
And they're also using
this in combination

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.560
with the Cloud
Translation API, which I'm

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:14.915
going to talk about later on.

00:11:14.915 --> 00:11:16.540
So there's a lot of
potential use cases

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.831
where you could combine
different machine learning APIs

00:11:18.831 --> 00:11:19.810
together.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:23.140
So in cases where the matches
don't speak the same language,

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:26.050
they'll use the Speech API
to transcribe the audio

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:29.035
and then the Translation
API to translate that text.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.790
The best way to experience
a Speech API is with a demo.

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:37.800
Before I get into it, I want
to explain a bit how it works.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:39.410
So we're going to
make a recording.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:41.270
I wrote a bash script.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:42.950
We're going to use
SoX to do that.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.530
It's a command line
utility for audio.

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:47.630
So what we'll do is
we'll record our audio.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:50.090
We'll create an API
request in a JSON file.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:51.980
And we'll send it
to the Speech API.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.026
And then we'll see
the JSON response.

00:11:54.026 --> 00:11:56.025
So if we could go ahead
and switch to the demo--

00:12:00.502 --> 00:12:03.350
OK, let me make the
font a little bigger.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:07.440
So I'm going to call my
file with bash request.sh.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.820
And it press "Press Enter
when you're ready to record."

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:13.220
It's going to ask me to record
a five-second audio file.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:13.810
So here we go.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:19.230
I built a Cloud Speech
API demo using SoX.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.880
OK, so this is the JSON request
file that it just created.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.380
We need to tell the Speech
API the encoding type.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.750
In this case, we're
using FLAC encoding,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.880
the sample rate in hertz.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:34.210
The language code is optional.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.210
If you leave it out, it
will default to English.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.127
Otherwise, you need to tell it
what language your audio is in.

00:12:39.127 --> 00:12:40.710
And then the speech
context, I'm going

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.030
to talk about that
in a little bit.

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:44.850
So I'm going to
call the Speech API.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.040
It's making a curl request.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.740
And let's see how it did.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.160
OK, so it did pretty good.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.064
It said, "I built a Cloud
Speech API demo using socks."

00:12:55.064 --> 00:12:56.730
But you'll notice it
got the wrong "SoX"

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:59.230
because "SoX" is a proper noun.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.300
It was 89% confident
that it got this correct.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.190
That even was able to
get "API" as an acronym.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.459
So I mentioned this speech
context parameter before.

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:11.250
And what this actually
lets you do is let's

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.100
say you have a
proper noun or a word

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:15.990
that you're expecting
your application that's

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:19.740
unique that you wouldn't expect
the API to recognize normally.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.996
You can actually pass
it as a parameter,

00:13:21.996 --> 00:13:23.370
and it'll look
out for that word.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.490
So I'm going to hop
on over to Sublime,

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:30.690
and I'm going to add "SoX"
as a phrase so look out for.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:33.180
And let's see if it's
able to identify it.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:36.460
I'm going to say the
same thing again.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:38.650
I'm going to record.

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.160
I built a Cloud Speech
API demo using SoX.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:48.250
And we can see it's now
got that phrase in there.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:49.600
And we will call the Speech API.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:55.930
And it was able to
get it correctly

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.720
using the phrases parameter,
which is pretty cool.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.960
Just one REST API
request, and we are easily

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:05.920
transcribing an audio file,
even with a unique entity.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:08.379
And you can also pass
the API audio files

00:14:08.379 --> 00:14:09.670
in over 80 different languages.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.280
You just need to tell it, again,
the language code that you'd

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:13.600
like it to transcribe.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.970
So that is the Speech API.

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:17.695
You can hop back to the slides.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:24.180
So we've just
transcribed our audio.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.040
We have text.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.700
So what happens if you want to
do more analysis on that text?

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:33.320
And that is where the Natural
Language API comes into play.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.870
It lets you extract entities,
sentiment, and syntax

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.352
from text.

00:14:39.352 --> 00:14:41.310
A company that's using
the Natural Language API

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:42.930
is called Wootric.

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.570
And they are a customer
feedback platform

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:48.420
to help businesses improve
their customer service.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.630
And they do this by collecting
millions of survey responses

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.340
each week.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.310
A little more detail
on how it works

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:58.320
is if you look at that
box in the top right--

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:00.386
so a customer would place
this on different pages

00:15:00.386 --> 00:15:01.260
throughout their app.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:03.135
Maybe if you're a
developer, you would see it

00:15:03.135 --> 00:15:04.260
on a documentation page.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.050
And it would ask you to rate
your experience on that page

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:10.110
from 0 to 10, which is what
we call the Net Promoter

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:11.700
Score, NPS.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.440
So they gather that
score, and then they

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.380
give you some open
feedback to expand on what

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.490
you thought of the experience.

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.490
So as you can imagine,
it's pretty easy for them

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.282
to average out the Net
Promoter Score among tons

00:15:23.282 --> 00:15:24.240
of different responses.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.370
But what's much more
difficult is looking

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:28.026
at that open-ended feedback.

00:15:28.026 --> 00:15:30.150
And that's where they use
the Natural Language API.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.090
And they actually made
use of all three methods

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.260
in the Natural Language API.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:39.510
They used intimate analysis to
calibrate the numbered score

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:43.540
that users gave with their
feedback to see if it aligned.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:47.550
And then they use the entity and
syntax annotation to figure out

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.460
what the subject
was of the feedback

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:52.290
and then route it accordingly.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.942
So maybe somebody was
unhappy about pricing.

00:15:54.942 --> 00:15:56.400
Then they could
route that feedback

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:00.390
to the necessary person
and respond pretty fast.

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.370
So using the Natural
Language API,

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:04.530
they were able to route
and respond to feedback

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.690
in near real-time
rather than having

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.700
someone need to
read each response,

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.940
classify it, and then route it.

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:16.450
So let's look at each of the
methods of the Natural Language

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:18.325
API in a bit more detail.

00:16:18.325 --> 00:16:20.200
As I mentioned, I'm a
big "Harry Potter" fan.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.770
So I took this sentence from
JK Rowling's Wikipedia page.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.860
And let's see what happens
if we send this to the entity

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.650
extraction endpoint.

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:33.820
So it's able to pull these five
entities from the sentence.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:37.250
And the JSON we get
back looks like this.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:40.030
So what's interesting
here is JK Rowling's name

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:41.620
is written in three
different ways.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:43.300
Robert Galbraith is
actually a pen name

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.140
she used for a
later book series.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:49.280
And it's able to point all
of these to the same entity.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.492
So if you had things like
"San Francisco" and "SF,"

00:16:51.492 --> 00:16:53.200
it would point those
to the same entities

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:55.930
so that you could count the
different ways of mentioning

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:58.190
the same thing as
the same entity.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:01.180
So we can see it finds her
name, Joanne "Jo" Rowling.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:03.310
It tells you what
type of entity it is--

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:04.960
a person.

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.060
And then if the
entity has metadata,

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:08.750
it'll give you more
metadata about it.

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:13.569
So here we get an MID, which
maps to JK Rowling's Knowledge

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:14.890
Graph entry.

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:19.040
And then we get the Wikipedia
URL to the page about her.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:21.670
The JSON response look
similar for the other entities

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:22.740
we found--

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:23.280
British.

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:25.030
It maps it to a location.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:28.581
And then notice it connects it
to the United Kingdom Wikipedia

00:17:28.581 --> 00:17:29.080
URL.

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:32.590
So if it had instead said
"UK" or "United Kingdom,"

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:35.980
it would point it
to the same page.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.290
And then for "Harry
Potter," we also get person.

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:42.494
And we get the Wikipedia
page for that entity as well.

00:17:42.494 --> 00:17:43.660
So that's entity extraction.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:48.870
That's one method you could use
in the Natural Language API.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:51.000
The next one is
sentiment analysis.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.500
So this is a review
you might see.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:53.875
It says, "The food
was excellent,

00:17:53.875 --> 00:17:55.230
I would definitely go back."

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:56.610
And we get two things here.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:59.630
We get a score value,
which will tell us,

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.680
is the sentiment
positive or negative?

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:05.200
It's a value ranging
from negative 1 to 1.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.070
So we can see here it's
almost completely positive.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.570
And then magnitude
will tell you how

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:12.270
strong is the sentiment
regardless of being

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:13.970
positive or negative.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.350
This can range
from 0 to infinity.

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.330
And it's based on the
length of the text.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.880
So since this is a pretty
short block of text,

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.320
the value is pretty low.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.510
And then finally, you
can analyze syntax.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:29.051
So this method is
a bit more complex.

00:18:29.051 --> 00:18:33.800
It gets into the linguistic
details of a piece of text.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.757
It returns a bunch of
different data here.

00:18:35.757 --> 00:18:37.340
This visualization
is actually created

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:38.417
from the in-browser demo.

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:40.250
So if you want to try
it out in the browser,

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.140
you can create a
similar visualization

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:43.520
with your own text.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:47.810
And what it returns is on that
top row, those green arrows,

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.385
that's what we call a
dependency parse tree.

00:18:50.385 --> 00:18:52.760
And that will tell us how each
of the words in a sentence

00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:56.280
relate to each other,
which words they depend on.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.530
In the second row, we
see the orange row.

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:00.187
That's the parse
label, which tells us

00:19:00.187 --> 00:19:01.770
the role of each
word in the sentence.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:03.910
So we can see that
"helps"-- the sentence

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:07.050
is "The natural language API
helps us understand text."

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:09.230
"Helps" is the root verb.

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:11.234
"Us" is the nominal subject.

00:19:11.234 --> 00:19:13.650
We can see the role of all the
other words in the sentence

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.130
as well.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:18.710
That third row where we only
have one word is the lemma.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:20.790
We can see here it says "help."

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.420
And what that is is the
canonical form of the word.

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:27.000
So the canonical form
of "helps" is "help."

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.770
So this way, if
you're trying to count

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:31.710
how many times a
specific word occurs,

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.150
it won't count "helps" and
"help" as two different words.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.080
It'll consolidate
them into one word.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:39.880
And then in red, we
have the part of speech,

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.950
whether it's a noun, verb,
adjective, or punctuation.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:47.640
And then in blue, we have some
additional morphology details

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:48.847
on the word.

00:19:48.847 --> 00:19:51.180
There's more of these returned
for Spanish and Japanese,

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.244
which are the other two
languages that the API

00:19:53.244 --> 00:19:54.035
currently supports.

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:59.070
So the syntax
annotation feature might

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:00.990
be a little harder to
grasp when you might

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:02.676
use this in an application.

00:20:02.676 --> 00:20:04.800
So I wanted to show you a
demo specifically focused

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:06.570
on that feature.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:10.950
And for this demo, over the
course of the past few days--

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:12.280
oh, I think the--

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:12.890
there we go.

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:14.040
Mic is back.

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:16.040
I've been using the
Twitter Streaming API

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.730
to stream tweet
about Google Next.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:21.840
So I've streamed tweets with
the hashtag #googlenext17

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:24.900
and a couple other search terms
that are put in node server

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:27.149
that's running on
Compute Engine.

00:20:27.149 --> 00:20:28.440
And I'm streaming those tweets.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.300
So the Streaming API
gathers just a subset

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.820
of those tweets, not all the
tweets with that hashtag.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.590
And I'm sending the
text of the tweet

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.420
to the Natural Language API.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.380
And then I'm storing the
response in BigQuery.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:42.270
BigQuery's our big data
analytics warehouse tool.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.110
It let's you do analytics
on really large data sets.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:47.100
So I'm storing it in BigQuery.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:49.140
And then from there,
I can gather some data

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.020
on the parts of
speech in a sentence.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:54.630
So I can find, for example,
the most common adjectives

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:57.720
that people are using to
tweet about Google Next.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:59.250
So if we could go to the demo--

00:21:02.340 --> 00:21:02.840
cool.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:06.290
So this is the BigQuery web
UI which lets you run queries

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:08.410
directly in the browser.

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:10.160
So here, I just did a
limit 10 to show you

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:11.630
what the table looks like.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:15.200
I think I've got about 6,000
to 7,000 tweets in here so far.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.790
So here I'm collecting the
ID of the tweet, the text,

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:22.330
the created at, how many
followers the user has,

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:25.430
what hashtags it finds that's
returned from the Twitter API.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:28.580
And then I've got this giant
JSON sring of the response

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:30.590
from the Natural Language API.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.210
So you're probably
wondering, how

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:35.340
am I going to write a
SQL query to parse that?

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:38.240
Well, BigQuery has a feature
called user-defined functions,

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.760
which lets you write custom
JavaScript functions that you

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:43.310
can run on rows in your table.

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:47.480
So over here, I've
got a query that's

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:50.120
going to run on every
tweet in this table.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.285
And my JavaScript
function is right here.

00:21:52.285 --> 00:21:53.660
And what it's
going to do is it's

00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:56.900
going to count all
of the adjectives

00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:59.790
and then return that
in my output table.

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:02.280
So if I run this
query here, it's

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:03.930
running this custom
JavaScript function

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:05.639
on all the tweets
in my table, which I

00:22:05.639 --> 00:22:06.930
think is about 6,000 right now.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:10.150
It ran pretty fast, and I'm
not using cached results.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.090
So let's take a look.

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:15.900
We've got 405 uses of the
word "more," "new," "great,"

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.936
"good," "late," "awesome."

00:22:18.936 --> 00:22:21.260
You can see some more here.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:24.800
So that's one example of a use
case for the syntax annotation

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:28.400
feature of the
Natural Language API.

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:29.865
You can go back to the slides.

00:22:35.333 --> 00:22:38.146
So the Natural Language API
lets you do analysis on text.

00:22:38.146 --> 00:22:40.270
One other thing that you
might want to do with text

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:41.350
is translate it.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:44.470
You likely have users of your
application all over the world.

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:48.100
It'd be useful to translate
it into their host language.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:51.040
And the Translation API
exposes the functionality

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:52.780
of Google Translate
to developers

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:55.960
and lets you translate
text in 100-plus languages.

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.334
And for a second, let's
talk about Google Translate.

00:22:58.334 --> 00:22:59.750
I'm a big fan of
Google Translate.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:02.006
Has anyone here used it before?

00:23:02.006 --> 00:23:03.660
It looks like a lot of people.

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:05.180
I use it when I
travel all the time.

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.559
So a couple of months ago,
I was on a trip to Japan.

00:23:07.559 --> 00:23:09.600
I was at a restaurant
where nobody spoke English,

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:11.310
and I really wanted
to order octopus.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:12.810
So I typed it into
Google translate.

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:14.970
It turns out the word
for that is "tako,"

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:16.600
which confused me a little bit.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.420
I didn't to order
an octopus taco,

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:20.626
although maybe
that would be good.

00:23:20.626 --> 00:23:22.500
So I just showed the
person at the restaurant

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.730
my Google translate app,
successfully got my octopus.

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.219
That's a picture
of it right there.

00:23:28.219 --> 00:23:30.510
But likely, you want to do
more than translate the word

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:31.739
for octopus.

00:23:31.739 --> 00:23:33.780
And that's why we have
the Translation API, which

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:37.020
lets you translate text
in your application

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:38.890
in many different languages.

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:40.920
And Airbnb is an example
of a company that's

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.740
using the Translation API.

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:47.340
And what you might not know
is that 60% of Airbnb bookings

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:50.070
connects people that are using
the app in different languages

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:52.920
because people use Airbnb a
lot, especially when they travel

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.350
internationally.

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:57.270
And so for all of
those connections,

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:00.210
they're using to translate API
to translate not only listings,

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:03.930
but also reviews
and conversations

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:05.700
into the person's
host's language.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.290
And they found that
this significantly

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:09.450
improves a guest
likelihood to book

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:13.030
if it's translated into
their host language.

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:15.570
And that's one example of
someone using the Translation

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:17.572
API.

00:24:17.572 --> 00:24:19.030
It's pretty
self-explanatory, but I

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:21.029
wanted to show you a code
snippet of how easy it

00:24:21.029 --> 00:24:22.500
is to call the API.

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.970
This is some Python code
that's making a request

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:26.590
to the Translation API.

00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:30.160
And you can see here we just
create a translate client.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:33.910
And then we pass it the
phrase we'd like to translate,

00:24:33.910 --> 00:24:34.780
the target language.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.790
It'll detect the
original language for us.

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:42.150
And then we can
print the result.

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:44.150
And one thing we've added
to the Translation API

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:47.250
recently is neural
machine translation.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:50.690
And this greatly improves the
underlying translation model.

00:24:50.690 --> 00:24:52.250
And basically, the
way it works is

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:54.650
with first-generation
translation,

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:57.200
which is what we had
before, it was translating

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:59.130
each word in a
sentence separately.

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:01.370
So let's say you
had a dictionary

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.457
and you were looking
up word for word

00:25:03.457 --> 00:25:05.540
each sentence in the
dictionary and translating it

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:08.960
without understanding the
context around that word, that

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.460
works pretty well.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:12.200
But what neural machine
translation does

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:14.840
is it actually looks at
the surrounding words,

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:17.780
and it's able to
understand the context

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:19.430
of the word in the sentence.

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:23.124
And it's able to produce much
higher quality translations.

00:25:23.124 --> 00:25:25.790
There's a great "New York Times"
article with a lot more details

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:26.810
on how that model works.

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:29.024
You can find it at
that Bitly link there.

00:25:29.024 --> 00:25:30.440
If anyone wants
to take a picture,

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:31.940
I'll leave it up for a second.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.490
And just to show you
some of the improvements

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.444
that neural machine
translation brings--

00:25:41.444 --> 00:25:42.360
this is a lot of text.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:42.860
I know.

00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:45.239
But what I did is
I took a paragraph

00:25:45.239 --> 00:25:47.030
from the Spanish version
of "Harry Potter."

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:49.390
So this is the original text
that the Spanish translator

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:50.920
wrote.

00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:53.380
And then I showed you in
first-generation translation

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:55.390
in the middle how
it'll translate that

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:58.719
to English, and then in
neural machine translation all

00:25:58.719 --> 00:26:00.010
the way on the right-hand side.

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:01.897
And I bolded the improvements.

00:26:01.897 --> 00:26:03.230
So we can look at a few of them.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:04.990
The first bold word is--

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:06.200
it says, "which made drills."

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.320
It's describing the
company where he works.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.060
And then neural
machine translation

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:12.920
is able to change that
word to "manufactured,"

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:16.910
which is much more specific to
the context of the sentence.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:19.400
Another example is
if we look at where

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:21.250
it's describing
Mrs. Dursley's neck,

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:23.200
the first generation
says "almost twice longer

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:24.389
than usual."

00:26:24.389 --> 00:26:26.680
In the second version, it
says "almost twice as long as

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:28.750
usual," which is a
slight improvement.

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:30.530
And then if we
look at the bottom,

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:34.150
it goes from "fence of the
garden" to "garden fence."

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:37.300
And then in the last
example, the first generation

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:38.752
used the pronoun "their."

00:26:38.752 --> 00:26:40.210
And then neural
machine translation

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:43.030
is able to identify the
correct pronoun "her"

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:44.810
more specifically.

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:47.260
So just a quick example
highlighting some improvements

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:50.450
that neural machine translation
brings to the Translation API.

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:56.610
And on its own, the Translation
API is pretty self-explanatory,

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.470
but I wanted to show you a
small demo of a Python script

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:03.290
I wrote of how to combine
different APIs together.

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:06.430
So in this demo, it'll take
three types of text input.

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:10.840
It could take either raw text,
audio, or an image of text.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:13.340
And then we'll pass it through
the Natural Language API.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:15.040
And then finally,
we'll translate it

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:16.399
into a few languages.

00:27:16.399 --> 00:27:18.190
And then we'll translate
it back to English

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:20.150
so that you can see the result.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:23.080
So if we could switch
back to the demo--

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:28.360
it looks like it's up here.

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:30.320
So I'm going to run the script--

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:32.640
python textify.py.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:34.182
And it's going to
tell me we're going

00:27:34.182 --> 00:27:36.098
to send some text to the
Natural Language API.

00:27:36.098 --> 00:27:37.980
It supports English,
Spanish, and Japanese.

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:39.021
And I have three options.

00:27:39.021 --> 00:27:41.220
I can either type my
text, record a file,

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:42.240
or send a photo of text.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:44.340
So I'm going to type some text.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:48.820
I'm going to say "We are
using the Translation API.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:51.830
It is awesome."

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:53.720
And we got a bunch
of data back here.

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:55.850
So this is what
the JSON response

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:57.650
looks like just for one token.

00:27:57.650 --> 00:27:59.990
I didn't want to print
the whole JSON blob here.

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:01.525
This is just for the token "we."

00:28:01.525 --> 00:28:02.900
This is all the
data it returned.

00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:05.660
So it tells us it's a pronoun.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.642
And a lot of these part
of speech morphology data

00:28:08.642 --> 00:28:10.100
is going to be
unknown for English,

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:11.610
but it relates to
other languages

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.130
that the API supports.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:16.730
But it is able to tell us
that it is a plural token

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.280
and it is in first person.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:21.470
And it is the nominal
subject of the sentence.

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:23.900
And we ran some
sentiment analysis on it.

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:25.775
It says you seem very happy.

00:28:25.775 --> 00:28:28.880
It was an excited sentence.

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:30.680
And it tells us the
entities it found.

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:32.960
So it found Translation API.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.270
And the way the entity
analysis endpoint works

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:37.790
is it's able to identify
entities even if they

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:39.170
don't have a Wikipedia URL.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:41.210
So Translation API
doesn't, but it's still

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:43.400
able to pull this
out as an entity.

00:28:43.400 --> 00:28:46.460
So if we were, for example,
building an app to maybe route

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:50.030
customer feedback, we could
say, OK, this feedback

00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:52.430
is asking about the
Translation API.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.500
And then we could route it
to the appropriate person.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:57.830
So now we're going to
translate this text.

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:01.960
And let's translate
it into Japanese.

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:02.460
There we go.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.580
So this is the version
translated into Japanese.

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:06.455
I'm guessing most of us
don't speak Japanese,

00:29:06.455 --> 00:29:08.330
so I've translated
it back to English.

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:11.170
And you can see that it
did a pretty good job.

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:13.850
So I'm going to run
the script once more.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:17.580
And [AUDIO OUT] use an image.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.600
So if we look over here, I've
got just a generic resume

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:21.620
image.

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:25.540
We're going to
pass it to the API.

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:29.230
So I'll clear the screen
and run the text again.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:31.190
We're going to send
a photo this time.

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:32.590
And it is resume.jpg.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:37.130
Sending it to the Vision API.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:42.050
And the Vision API
found, if we scroll up,

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:43.910
all this text in the image.

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:46.060
Using the new document
text extraction,

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:50.650
it was able to pull essentially
all the text from that resume.

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:52.960
There's an example of
what a token returned.

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:55.270
And it found all these
different entities.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:57.060
And now we can translate it.

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:59.430
Let's translate it to German.

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:01.450
It's a lot of text
there; I know.

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:03.850
But this is the resume
translated into German,

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:06.220
and then again back to English.

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:09.100
So just an example of how you
can combine multiple machine

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:13.000
learning APIs to do
some cool text analysis.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:17.400
And you can go back
to the slides now.

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:20.855
So that was the Translation API.

00:30:20.855 --> 00:30:22.480
And the last thing
I want to talk about

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:24.100
is the Video Intelligence API.

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:26.760
How many of you saw it
at the keynote yesterday?

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:28.310
Looks like most of you.

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:29.820
So the Video
Intelligence API lets

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:33.030
you understand your videos
entities at a shot, frame,

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:34.710
or video level.

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:37.320
So video level entities,
it will tell you,

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:39.870
at a high level, what
is this video about?

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:41.970
And then it'll give
you more granular data

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:46.270
on what is happening in
each scene of the video.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:48.740
A company that's
using this is Cantemo.

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:51.090
They're a media asset
management company.

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:53.720
So a company or a user
that has a lot of videos

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:56.120
would upload their
videos to Cantemo,

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:59.140
and Cantemo would help them
better understand those videos,

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:02.181
search their library,
transcode videos.

00:31:02.181 --> 00:31:04.430
And this is a quote from the
VP of product development

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:05.810
at Cantemo.

00:31:05.810 --> 00:31:08.901
He says, "Thanks to the Google
Cloud Video Intelligence API,

00:31:08.901 --> 00:31:11.150
we've been able to very
quickly process and understand

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:13.970
the content of video down
to the individual frame

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:16.760
with an impressively
rich taxonomy."

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:18.530
So they're using the
Video API to help

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:21.440
their customers better search
their own video libraries.

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:25.680
And I'm going to show you
the same demo that you

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.550
saw in the keynote, but we'll
look at a different video

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:29.800
and go into a bit more detail.

00:31:29.800 --> 00:31:36.369
So if we could switch back
to the demo, here's the API.

00:31:36.369 --> 00:31:37.910
And I've got a
different video than I

00:31:37.910 --> 00:31:40.150
showed in the keynote earlier.

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:42.980
This is a video that just shows
you a tour of the Google Paris

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:46.280
office and a little bit of
the neighborhood around it.

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:48.911
And I'll play the
first bit of it.

00:31:48.911 --> 00:31:50.770
It starts up by just
showing some frames.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:56.775
And then we'll get into a
tour of the neighborhood

00:31:56.775 --> 00:31:57.525
around the office.

00:32:02.050 --> 00:32:03.130
And then we go inside.

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:04.870
It interviews some employees.

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:06.347
I won't play the whole thing.

00:32:06.347 --> 00:32:08.430
But we can look at some
of the labels it returned.

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:12.010
So it's able to identify this
amusement ride, amusement park,

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:13.567
from the beginning.

00:32:13.567 --> 00:32:15.400
We know there's a bunch
of very short frames

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:16.691
in the beginning of that video.

00:32:16.691 --> 00:32:19.120
It's able to see
that it's a statue.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:21.025
If we look at the
fruit annotation,

00:32:21.025 --> 00:32:24.970
it identifies a basket
of fruit in this scene.

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:27.720
We can scroll down and look
at a couple more labels--

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:32.460
landscaping and cuisine.

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:33.870
We see people getting some food.

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:41.270
And school-- here it thinks
it's a school inside there.

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:44.790
So you can see we're able to get
pretty granular data on what's

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:47.880
happening in each
scene of the video.

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:50.490
And another thing the Video
Intelligence API lets us do

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:53.400
is search a large video library.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:55.080
So if we're a media
publisher, we've

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:58.140
got petabytes of video data
sitting in storage buckets.

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.900
It's otherwise pretty hard
to search a large library

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:01.980
of video content.

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.230
You'd have to manually
watch the videos looking

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.480
for a particular clip if
you want to create, say,

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:09.510
like a highlight reel
of a specific content

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:11.354
within your library.

00:33:11.354 --> 00:33:13.020
Intelligence API makes
this pretty easy,

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:16.740
because as you can see, all the
data we get back on this video,

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:19.500
we can get that back on all the
videos in our library, which

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:21.910
makes it pretty easy to just
search for a specific entity

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:24.870
within our library.

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:26.520
So as I showed you
in the keynote,

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:27.675
one example is let's say--

00:33:27.675 --> 00:33:29.550
actually, first, let me
show you the library.

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:32.190
So we've got a bunch of
videos here, as you can see.

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:36.131
And let's say we'd like to
search for all our baseball

00:33:36.131 --> 00:33:36.630
videos.

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:44.110
We can see what
we get back here.

00:33:44.110 --> 00:33:48.050
And it shows us this video is
almost entirely about baseball.

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:49.790
This one has fewer
baseball clips.

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:53.630
And we can point to all
of them specifically.

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:58.170
And then in this one, we
see that moment from the--

00:33:58.170 --> 00:33:58.980
not playing.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:02.360
Let me try refreshing the page.

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:02.860
There we go.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:05.720
We see that moment from the Year
in Search video from last year

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:07.178
when the Cubs won
the World Series.

00:34:07.178 --> 00:34:10.310
I'm from Chicago, so I was
pretty excited about that.

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:12.488
One more search that I
showed you before is we

00:34:12.488 --> 00:34:14.404
can find all of the beach
clips in our videos.

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:19.960
So here, it's easy to, if we
wanted to create a highlight

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:22.096
reel, if we were really
missing the beach,

00:34:22.096 --> 00:34:23.679
see all the beach
clips in our videos.

00:34:23.679 --> 00:34:26.889
It'd be super easy to do this
using the Video Intelligence

00:34:26.889 --> 00:34:29.004
API.

00:34:29.004 --> 00:34:31.170
Now, since most of you saw
this demo in the keynote,

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:34.016
I wanted to talk a little bit
more about how I built it.

00:34:34.016 --> 00:34:35.224
So you can go back to slides.

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:41.380
It was built by
me and Alex Wolfe.

00:34:41.380 --> 00:34:43.963
If you like the UI, you should
give him a shoutout on Twitter,

00:34:43.963 --> 00:34:44.980
@alexwolfe.

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:46.489
He would appreciate it.

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:47.920
So we worked on this together.

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:51.409
And this is an architecture
diagram of how it works.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:54.299
So the video API processing
is being done on the back end.

00:34:54.299 --> 00:34:56.590
And the way it works is you
pass the Video Intelligence

00:34:56.590 --> 00:35:00.640
API a Google Cloud
Storage URL of your video.

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:02.860
And then it'll run the
analysis on that video.

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.047
So I have a Cloud
Storage bucket where

00:35:05.047 --> 00:35:06.130
I'm storing all my videos.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:09.550
And I've got a cloud function
listening on that bucket.

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:11.350
And that cloud function
will be triggered

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:13.720
anytime a new file is
added to the bucket.

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:15.069
It will check if it's a video.

00:35:15.069 --> 00:35:17.110
If it is, it'll send it
to the Video Intelligence

00:35:17.110 --> 00:35:18.892
API for processing.

00:35:18.892 --> 00:35:20.350
And one cool thing
about the API is

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:23.980
you can pass it the output
URL of the URL of a file

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:26.260
you'd like it to write
the JSON response to

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:27.971
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:35:27.971 --> 00:35:29.470
So I've got a
separate cloud storage

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:32.680
bucket where I'm storing all
the video annotation JSON

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:33.970
responses.

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:37.930
And the API automatically writes
all of my video annotations

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:39.154
to that bucket.

00:35:39.154 --> 00:35:40.570
So the front end
of my application

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:42.403
doesn't have to call
the video API directly.

00:35:42.403 --> 00:35:45.220
It's already got all that
metadata in two separate cloud

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:46.840
storage buckets.

00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:48.340
And the front end
of the application

00:35:48.340 --> 00:35:52.810
is a Node.js application
built on Google App Engine.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:54.865
That's a little bit
about how the demo works.

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:01.000
And this is more
granularly what the JSON

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.460
response looks like from
the Video Intelligence API.

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:06.580
So this is a video of a
tour of the White House.

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:08.590
And at this particular
point in time,

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:11.650
it identifies the label
"Bird's-eye view."

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:13.960
And it's able to tell us
the start time and end

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:16.780
time in microseconds of
where that label appears

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:17.400
in the video.

00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:21.380
And it also returns a confidence
score ranging from 0 to 1,

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:24.970
which will tell us how confident
is the API that it successfully

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:26.980
identified this as
bird's-eye view.

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:29.120
In this case, it is 96%.

00:36:29.120 --> 00:36:31.600
So it is pretty confident that
this is a bird's-eye view.

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:35.950
And then one more
snippet of this video,

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:38.230
a portrait of George
Washington-- and it's

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:41.450
able to successfully identify
that this is a portrait.

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:42.910
It tells us the
start time and end

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:46.120
time of where that is in the
video, along with a confidence

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:46.840
score--

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:48.430
83%.

00:36:48.430 --> 00:36:50.320
So just an example of
what the JSON looks

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:52.940
like that you get back from
the Video Intelligence API.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:57.224
That wraps up my
tour of the APIs.

00:36:57.224 --> 00:36:59.140
And if you all want to
start using them today,

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:02.000
you can go to the try-it pages.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:04.210
So for each of the
API product pages,

00:37:04.210 --> 00:37:06.100
as I showed you
with Vision, there's

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:09.160
an in-browser demo where you can
try out all the APIs directly

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.320
in the browser before
writing any code

00:37:11.320 --> 00:37:14.030
to see if it's right for
you and your application.

00:37:14.030 --> 00:37:16.030
So I definitely recommend
checking that out.

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:19.450
I'll let you guys take a picture
of that page before I switch.

00:37:24.708 --> 00:37:31.120
OK, it looks like almost
everyone has got it.

